reds or millions of  documents  and ranks them from which one is most  important  and then returns to the user the most  relevant ones  i guess in simple terms  so  and everybody engages with information  which people processes each day it's on  google it's on youtube it's everywhere  and  you know back  like at its first beginning stages it  was focused on text  and articles  but then it grew to  it grew to accommodate for videos and  various kinds of media  such as chemical structures  and dna sequences which is how it's  applied to the medical industry  so there are two types of retrieval the  first one is exact match  it returns all documents that match  exactly is what the user entered using  boolean operators  partial match  doesn't require boolean operators and  it's a little  it's a little bit more different and  then the information retrieval process  um looks like this so the user poses a  query  and then the search engine matches the  query to the metadata and the metadata  of an article of a link it contains  typically the  author of an article the date it was  published  and  um the file size and this metadata is  used  to um search through the files a lot  more efficiently and a lot more quickly  rather than having to um  use other inefficient methods  so an important way to evaluate if a  search engine is efficient  is  how first of all if the system is used  second  what was what is it used for  specifically  and third how well did someone use the  system  and if it's very user friendly  and if it's accessible  and for are the users satisfied  you know did they find what they were  looking for did they have an easy time  finding it and five what factors are  associated with successful or  unsuccessful use of the system  so  you know is it clear within the  algorithm  if  successful use um  you know how how are you going to  measure that and it's important to have  an idea  of how you're going to measure success  and success  with one of these algorithms and  six did the system have an impact did it  make anything  any easier within a research process  and then a really common type of system  oriented evaluation is recall  it's the number of retrieved and  relevant documents  over the number of divided by the number  of relevant documents in a database  and  then there's precision which is it gives  a fraction of how many relevant  documents there are from a specified  number of documents that were retrieved  rather so this is a bit more  um focused in scope it's not it doesn't  go over the entire database it's just  over the documents that were  specifically obtained  so now that you got some background  information on this  on these concepts um  trekkovid is a collaboration that  applies these concepts  for  medical journals  and it's a collaboration between the  university of texas  the national institute of standards of  technology  oregon health and science university in  the u.s national library of medicine  and the allen institute for ai  and it's meant to examine various  information retrieval methods  and how efficient they can be and how  good they are at obtaining the most  relevant and accurate articles  so peer reviewed articles are the golden  standard when it comes to  searching for medical information  they're trusted by experts all across  the field  everybody  uses them  and  with so many articles and so many  studies coming out every single day  the most important question is how are  health experts supposed to keep up with  what journal articles are most relevant  in an efficient  manner so how this task will improve  information retrieval is to reduce the  amount of time it takes to look for  reliable data  it also aims to search for the most  relevant reports by using comparison  algorithms  and ultimately aims to prevent the  spread of misinformation you know you  don't want a search engine to show you  data from  you know last week when it could show  you a report that came out an hour ag