jects i've been working on  this summer  nearly half of adults in the united  states have high blood pressure also  called hypertension  according to the cdc only one in four  adults with hypertension have controlled  blood pressure uncontrolled blood  pressure increases the risk of heart  disease and stroke which are two of the  leading causes of of death for older  adults  although clinical guidelines for the  treatment of hypertension represent a  wide breadth of biomedical knowledge the  implementation of these guidelines is  still inconsistent and resource  intensive  guidelines often conflict on the  appropriate diagnosis and treatment of  hypertension even definitions for what  blood pressure measurements constitute  stage 1 and stage 2 hypertension are  varied  these inconsis  consistencies require leveraging  provider experience gathering and  assessing knowledge and data manually  and attempting to integrate many  distinct factors and options often  resulting in inadequate hypertension  management for patients  additionally similarly to many common  conditions there is a plethora of  decisions made from diagnosis to  treatment  decisions that despite involving health  care providers are largely dependent on  the individual patient for example a  patient's other pre-existing health  conditions side effects and demographic  factors can significantly influence the  approach needed for effective treatment  in addition behavioral and lifestyle  factors such as alcohol consumption and  physical activity can also substantially  affect treatment and outcome  the ability to create computable and  personalized guideline-based  recommendations would not only shorten  timelines and reduce required resources  but would also improve overall care  as such the high blood pressure team has  been developing a patient-facing  clinical decision support or cds tool  that translates hypertension guidelines  into practice using clinical query  language or cql modules  these modules incorporate personalized  patient preferences through direct usage  by patients  this approach elicits the information  needed for patient facing clinical  decision support as well as  appropriately allows patients to  self-assess treatment progress and  adherence through a workflow sensitive  solution  specific innovations in this project  include the use of blood pressure sets  to match guidelines a counseling goal  and preference system for  non-pharmacologic recommendations and  specific patient and care team versions  of the recommendations  as an integral part of this project we  created recommendations that patients  will be given based on their engagement  with the tool  in total we use eight clinical  guidelines that encompass 71  recommendations as the foundation for  the logic behind six different workflows  the diagram here shows a brief overview  of how the app works  when a provider orders home blood  pressure monitoring the patient is  introduced to the app which will give  them different recommendations that  allow them to take action  the data that is pulled in and used to  drive the recommendations comes from  electronic health records as well as  engagement with the app itself  clinical quality framework or cqf ruler  uses cql to encode the logic to generate  cards which are the recommendations that  patients and care teams are shown  over this past summer we have been  working mostly on developing the initial  diagnosis and monitoring workflow as  well as the non-pharmacologic  interventions workflow which will be  shown on the next slide  the initial diagnosis and monitoring  workflow as shown here makes an initial  evaluation based on a patient's set of  blood pressure readings and then shows  the patient a recommendation associated  with this evaluation  for patients with high blood pressure  who have not been diagnosed with  hypertension the tool considers a  possible diagnosis for the patient and  the patient is recommended to reach out 