reds or millions of  documents  and ranks them from which one is most  important  and then returns to the user the most  relevant ones  i guess in simple terms  so  and everybody engages with information  which people processes each day it's on  google it's on youtube it's everywhere  and  you know back  like at its first beginning stages it  was focused on text  and articles  but then it grew to  it grew to accommodate for videos and  various kinds of media  such as chemical structures  and dna sequences which is how it's  applied to the medical industry  so there are two types of retrieval the  first one is exact match  it returns all documents that match  exactly is what the user entered using  boolean operators  partial match  doesn't require boolean operators and  it's a little  it's a little bit more different and  then the information retrieval process  um looks like this so the user poses a  query  and then the search engine matches the  query to the metadata and the metadata  of an article of a link it contains  typically the  author of an article the date it was  published  and  um the file size and this metadata is  used  to um search through the files a lot  more efficiently and a lot more quickly  rather than having to um  use other inefficient methods  so an important way to evaluate if a  search engine is efficient  is  how first of all if the system is used  second  what was what is it used for  specifically  and third how well did someone use the  system  and if it's very user friendly  and if it's accessible  and for are the users satisfied  you know did they find what they were  looking for did they have an easy time  finding it and five what factors are  associated with successful or  unsuccessful use of the system  so  you know is it clear within the  algorithm  if  successful use um  you know how how are you going to  measure that and it's important to have  an idea  of how you're going to measure success  and success  with one of these algorithms and  six did the system have an impact did it  make anything  any easier within a research process  and then a really common type of system  oriented evaluation is recall  it's the number of retrieved and  relevant documents  over the number of divided by the number  of relevant documents in a database  and  then there's precision which is it gives  a fraction of how many relevant  documents there are from a specified  number of documents that were retrieved  rather so this is a bit more  um focused in scope it's not it doesn't  go over the entire database it's just  over the documents that were  specifically obtained  so now that you got some background  information on this  on these concepts um  trekkovid is a collaboration that  applies these concepts  for  medical journals  and it's a collaboration between the  university of texas  the national institute of standards of  technology  oregon health and science university in  the u.s national library of medicine  and the allen institute for ai  and it's meant to examine various  information retrieval methods  and how efficient they can be and how  good they are at obtaining the most  relevant and accurate articles  so peer reviewed articles are the golden  standard when it comes to  searching for medical information  they're trusted by experts all across  the field  everybody  uses them  and  with so many articles and so many  studies coming out every single day  the most important question is how are  health experts supposed to keep up with  what journal articles are most relevant  in an efficient  manner so how this task will improve  information retrieval is to reduce the  amount of time it takes to look for  reliable data  it also aims to search for the most  relevant reports by using comparison  algorithms  and ultimately aims to prevent the  spread of misinformation you know you  don't want a search engine to show you  data from  you know last week when it could show  you a report that came out an hour ago  you always want to make sure that the  comparison  um is as up-to-date as possible  and what is being accounted for in this  experiment so the first one is how to  choose the content to search through you  know what are you gonna test your  information retrieval algorithm on  and in this project it was tested on a  coven 19 data set of research papers  from the um nih  and then the second is how do you  develop a search engine in such a short  matter of time  and to answer that is to build and ex  build it upon an existing search engine  so in this case it's through pi cerini  and anserini which are data retrieval  toolkits  available on github  and cerrini is a retrieval toolkit from  the lucine search engine library it's  open source  and it has a lot of resources with um  multi-stage ranking  and in pi cerrini is  a more user-friendly interface to  anserini  it's  very efficient to use with first stage  retrieval  and it includes queries and relevance  judgments and indexes and evaluations  and other questions that are being  accounted for in this experiment is what  are the appropriate information  retrieval modalities for this experiment  how do you optimize a search engine for  more specific situations  you know because some people can just  enter some keywords and they'll get  results back but others other users will  enter something much more specific  so how will it accommodate for that  and further how can existing data be  used to improve the search engine  so how can  we potentially teach a search engine to  improve upon itself each time  other questions that are being accounted  for  is  if event specific training data can be  created fast enough to have an impact  how can you quantitatively measure  a search engine's efficiency  and  when comparing different types of search  algorithms  what are the what characteristics are  the most important to compare  and finally how does one qualitative  qualitatively evaluate the search engine  so the procedure for this project was  that participants were giving a corpus  which was the data set of all of the  documents to evaluate and they were also  giving a list of topics  that they would enter in as queries to  see  what documents would come up every time  they searched it up on  on their information retrieval system  and participants have one week to create  their own information retrieval system  and run one thousand results per topic  and after that results are gathered and  manually evaluated  and judges rank how relevant each topic  is  so in this experiment um  so once everything is evaluated  or once the judges got to the  evaluations  they went through each paper  and  they had um they had to sort if it was  relevant partially relevant or not  relevant  and this skill was used to determine um  how  this algorithm  how accurate it was and it also helped  them compare  um  compare them with one another  and here it shows on  for each topic  how relevant  um  each of the searches were  and here are the judgments that go with  them  and for the technique that was used for  this experiment was a pulling technique  so what they did was that  in order to save time they got the top  rank results from each participant  and then judges manually ranked how if  you know if it was  the best  document that the search engine could  have gotten or if it wasn't relevant or  if it was  or if it just didn't have anything to do  with the search query  so a difficulty that comes with this  project is  you know it's just the massive reports  that come in each day  it's really hard to maintain a current  data set and  it's also you know  if you want to stay current you have to  download it over and over again and you  have to update it and add more articles  and more  new statistics each day  and the second was the lack of qualified  judges to manually assess each article  these judges are experts in their field  these judges  um  and also need to make the time to judge  all of these different articles  so that's one difficulty that came with  this  and in hopes of solving these  difficulties um  you know the first is just repeating the  procedure and seeing um if anything if  any other issues or if any other  potential solutions come up  and it's with five topics each time five  new topics  and then there's also the possibility of  including machine learning techniques  in order to lessen the need of human  judges  which would be by um you know having the  search engine  build its own criteria and its own  standards on its own  and finally some personal takeaways that  i got from this project  was that i obtained a greater  understanding on information retrieval  methods  and i also got a greater insight on the  statistical methods that overlap with  bioinformatics  and i got experience with using github  and the repository and importing various  toolkits from it  and overall i just want to acknowledge  everyone who  um was a part of this project and i also  want to acknowledge dr hirsch  he um  provided a you know he helped a lot with  the background information of  this project and  he  also helped me understand a lot of these  concepts  a lot more better  you know they're very hard to grasp at  first but um  you know his explanations made them a  lot more easy  to um digest and i would also like to  thank dr chamberla  sorry dr chamberlain for from ohsu  um he was very helpful when it came to  syntax errors and any coding errors  that i encountered during this project  and i would also like to thank everybody  at demise for listening to my  presentation and for  giving me  uh this opportunity to  you know put my foot in the door when it  came to machine learning  and i'd also like to thank the  university at buffalo new york for  expanding my knowledge and the allen  institute for ai and microsoft research  for the funding on this project  thank you very much  great thank you  any questions from the audience  okay um  since um  since since