ing system while my  chart is a  patient-facing system  and these ehr systems are a subset of  healthit healthit having greatly  improved care coordination among  providers and the clarity and  accessibility of medical data for  non-mcc patients  however there is little evidence that  health it has improved patient care for  mcc patients  the thought process behind this project  is that if we improve health i.t we may  be able to maximize benefits for mcc  patients  project goal  the project goal is to improve the  quality of care for mcc patients  especially those with chronic kidney  disease or ckd by improving ehr systems  the proposed solution for reaching this  goal was to build the ecare app an app  which will work in existing ehr  workflows will serve as a tool for both  providers and patients that contains the  patient's comprehensive care plan and  all of their medical data in one place  will enhance patient understanding of  their medical data and will emphasize  patient goals to providers  collaborators  there were many collaborating  institutions involved in this project  on the left there's a screenshot of a  slide from a deck of training slides  that i will introduce later  that summarizes the collaborators and  the roles very well  the niddk which is part of the nih  funded app development and cognitive  helped with the actual building of the  app  arc funded the testing and evaluation of  the app while rti international and ohsu  did the testing and evaluation  throughout this summer i attended many  zoom and webex meetings almost all of  them being meetings that recurred  frequently and got to experience and be  a part of the coordination that has to  happen between these collaborating  institutions on tasks deadlines and such  however there's one other resource that  was instrumental in this project and  that resource is known as fast  healthcare interoperability resources or  fire for short  and i might not be explaining this  perfectly but as one of my team members  on this project matt explained to me  code usually has to be rewritten for  each ehr system that uses it because ehr  systems almost always interact with and  store medical data in different ways  but fire solves this problem with its  framework as it standardizes data  formats and interactions so that ehr  code can be used on a variety of  platforms without having to be rewritten  fire is part of what makes this project  innovative populating the e-care plan  app with data from epic  the bottom right picture shows the fire  workflow  methods  first both provider and patient patient  facing versions of the ecare plan app  were built  here is a screenshot of what the health  status evaluation and outcomes page of  the app looks like in the provider  facing app to the left  and to the right  is a screenshot of the corresponding  page in the patient facing app  next a lot of preparation for usability  testing of the app was done  but before we get into that however  i wanted to briefly introduce what this  usability testing entails  it entails having facilitators such as  myself and my team member taylor  observing providers walking through the  app and getting their feedback on it  in preparation for this usability  testing a simulation patient dubbed  patricia noel was designed to be the  archetypal patient that would have this  app as part of their care  she has mcc advanced ckd and multiple  care teams across healthcare settings  the team the ecare  app team that is created three different  progressive medical scenarios for  patricia noel in order to test all  aspects of the app  to the right you can see a screenshot of  patricia's scenarios complete with  medications referrals and more  in addition because of her medical  conditions and medical data when she is  used to test the app the team will  immediately know if the ecare plan app  isn't working the way it should  and since i use these terms quite often  throughout the remainder of this  