e for those who don't have as much  experience with it as well as how  helpful the app is and would be for  patients with mcc  and this usability testing was done in  the app's test and live environments  the test environment for the  provider-facing app can be accessed  through epic poc and the test  environment for the patient-facing app  can be accessed through mychart poc  both environments are populated with  patricia noel's medical data  the provider facing apps live  environment conversely is accessible  through epic production and is populated  with real patient data  the real patients whose data was chosen  for usability testing had to meet the  following inclusion criteria  that they were the  participating providers patient so the  provider could tell us if something  wasn't showing on the app  and that they had mcc's at least one of  which is ckd so as to test the app to  the fullest extent  and before we move on to the next slide  i'd just like to quickly note that all  team members that interacted with real  patient data were trained to work with  it so that there would be no breaches of  privacy of any sort  also  the top picture shows how  the app can be accessed through epic  whether that's epic poc or epic  production  and the bottom screen shot shows how the  ecare plan app would be accessed through  mychart poc  and over the next few slides i provided  screenshots of the pages of the  provider-facing app  and i'll pause for a few seconds before  i continue on to the next slide but here  are the  first two pages of the app  and here are the next two and i have  these in order by the way  and here is the last page  and hopefully seeing the pages of the  app gives you a better sense of what we  were asking of providers doing during  your uat  findings  app strengths across the board the app  was found by participating providers to  be intuitive to navigate and use  these providers thought the app would  prove useful for more efficient and  effective